---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../config/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description={`Privacy Policy for ${site.name}, the personal reading tracker for iPhone.`}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container hero-inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy Policy</p>

      <h1>
        Your reading life<br />
        is yours.
      </h1>

      <p>
        Bookroom is designed around local storage, without accounts,
        advertising or behavioural tracking.
      </p>

      <span class="updated">
        Last updated: 13 August 2026
      </span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="policy">
    <div class="container policy-layout">
      <aside>
        <p class="eyebrow">At a glance</p>

        <div class="summary">
          <div>
            <strong>No account</strong>
            <span>Bookroom does not require you to create an account.</span>
          </div>

          <div>
            <strong>No advertising</strong>
            <span>Bookroom contains no advertising or advertising trackers.</span>
          </div>

          <div>
            <strong>No analytics</strong>
            <span>
              The current version does not use analytics or crash-reporting
              services.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div>
            <strong>Local-first</strong>
            <span>
              Your personal library and reading records are stored on your
              device.
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </aside>

      <article class="policy-content">
        <section>
          <h2>1. About this policy</h2>

          <p>
            This Privacy Policy explains how {site.name} handles information
            when you use the iPhone app.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bookroom is designed as a local-first reading tracker. It does not
            require an account and does not operate a Bookroom server containing
            your personal reading library.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>2. Information stored on your device</h2>

          <p>
            Bookroom stores information needed to provide its reading-tracking
            features locally on your device. Depending on the features you use,
            this may include:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>books in your library</li>
            <li>reading status and progress</li>
            <li>start and finish dates</li>
            <li>ratings</li>
            <li>private Shelf Memories</li>
            <li>rereading records</li>
            <li>shelf positions and horizontal book stacks</li>
            <li>decorations and room appearance settings</li>
            <li>recent book-search queries</li>
            <li>captured book-spine images</li>
            <li>a custom room-background image, if you choose one</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Bookroom does not implement its own cloud-sync service for this
            information.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>3. Book search and Open Library</h2>

          <p>
            Bookroom uses Open Library to provide book catalogue search,
            metadata and discovery features.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you perform a book search, the search text you enter is sent
            to Open Library so that matching books can be returned.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you scan an ISBN barcode, the detected ISBN is used as a book
            search and is therefore sent to Open Library.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bookroom may also request suggested books when you open the
            Add Book screen. To generate those suggestions, Bookroom may send
            a genre or author name taken from one of your recent library
            entries to Open Library.
          </p>

          <p>
            These requests necessarily communicate with Open Library over the
            internet. Open Library may receive ordinary network information
            associated with a request, such as your IP address and request
            metadata, according to its own practices.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bookroom does not send your complete library, Shelf Memories,
            ratings, reading progress or shelf arrangement to Open Library.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can learn more about Open Library at
            <a
              href="https://openlibrary.org/"
              rel="external noreferrer"
            >
              openlibrary.org
            </a>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>4. Book cover images</h2>

          <p>
            Search results and books in your library may contain cover artwork
            supplied by Open Library.
          </p>

          <p>
            When Bookroom displays one of these covers, the image may be
            downloaded from Open Library's cover-image service. This creates a
            normal network request to that service.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>5. Camera and photos</h2>

          <p>
            Bookroom may request access to your camera for two features:
            scanning ISBN barcodes and capturing photographs of physical book
            spines.
          </p>

          <p>
            Captured spine images are stored locally by Bookroom. The current
            version does not automatically upload those photographs to a
            Bookroom server or third-party image-processing service.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can also choose an image from your photo library to use as a
            custom reading-room background. The selected image is copied into
            Bookroom's local storage. The current version does not automatically
            upload that image.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>6. Shelf Memories</h2>

          <p>
            Shelf Memories are private notes attached to completed reading
            records.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shelf Memories are stored with your local reading data. They are
            not included when Bookroom renders a shelf image for sharing and
            are not included in CSV library exports.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shelf Memories are included in a Full Backup because Full Backup is
            designed to restore your Bookroom data completely.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>7. Full Backup</h2>

          <p>
            Full Backup is initiated manually by you.
          </p>

          <p>
            A Full Backup can contain your books, reading history, ratings,
            Shelf Memories, shelf arrangement, room appearance, decoration
            placement, captured spine images and a custom room-background
            image.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once the backup is created, Bookroom presents the iOS sharing
            interface. You decide where the backup is saved or sent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because a Full Backup can contain private information and images,
            you should store it somewhere you trust.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>8. CSV import and export</h2>

          <p>
            CSV export is intended for library portability rather than complete
            Bookroom restoration.
          </p>

          <p>
            A CSV export may contain book information and reading information
            such as title, author, ISBN, status, format, current page, page
            count, publication year, genre, series information, cover URL,
            reading dates and rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            CSV exports do not contain Shelf Memories, captured spine images,
            room-background images or the complete visual arrangement of your
            shelf.
          </p>

          <p>
            CSV imports are files that you explicitly select from your device
            using the iOS file picker.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>9. Sharing your shelf</h2>

          <p>
            Bookroom can render images of your shelf or reading collection for
            you to share.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sharing is initiated by you through the iOS sharing interface.
            Bookroom does not automatically publish your shelf.
          </p>

          <p>
            Private Shelf Memories are not included in generated shelf-sharing
            images.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you choose to share content with another app or service, that
            destination may process the shared content according to its own
            privacy practices.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>10. Accounts, identity and tracking</h2>

          <p>
            Bookroom does not require a user account and does not ask you to
            provide a name, email address, gender, age or other profile
            information in order to use the app.
          </p>

          <p>
            The current version contains no advertising SDK, behavioural
            tracking SDK or cross-app tracking system.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bookroom does not link your reading data to a Bookroom user
            identity because no Bookroom account system exists.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>11. Analytics and crash reporting</h2>

          <p>
            The current version of Bookroom does not include a third-party
            analytics or crash-reporting service.
          </p>

          <p>
            If this changes in a future version, this Privacy Policy will be
            updated before such data collection is introduced.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>12. Support correspondence</h2>

          <p>
            If you contact support by email, the email address you contact us
            from and any information you choose to include in your message will
            be received so that the support request can be answered.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bookroom does not automatically send your library, Shelf Memories
            or photographs when you contact support.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>13. Changes to this policy</h2>

          <p>
            This policy may be updated when Bookroom gains new features,
            services or data-handling behaviour.
          </p>

          <p>
            The date at the top of this page will be changed when the policy is
            materially updated.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>14. Contact</h2>

          <p>
            Questions about privacy or Bookroom's handling of information can
            be sent to:
          </p>

          <p>
            <a class="contact-link" href={`mailto:${site.supportEmail}`}>
              {site.supportEmail}
            </a>
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-hero {
    padding: 190px 0 110px;
    background: var(--color-forest-950);
    color: var(--color-text-light);
  }

  .hero-inner {
    max-width: 850px;
  }

  .page-hero .eyebrow {
    color: var(--color-brass);
  }

  .page-hero h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(3.5rem, 7vw, 6rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: -0.045em;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .page-hero p:not(.eyebrow) {
    max-width: 650px;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }

  .updated {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    color: rgb(200 192 177 / 65%);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }

  .policy {
    padding: var(--section-space) 0;
    background: var(--color-cream-50);
  }

  .policy-layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(210px, 0.55fr) minmax(0, 1.45fr);
    gap: clamp(4rem, 10vw, 9rem);
  }

  aside {
    align-self: start;
    position: sticky;
    top: 2rem;
  }

  .summary {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-cream-200);
  }

  .summary > div {
    padding: 1.25rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-cream-200);
  }

  .summary strong {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  .summary span {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
    color: var(--color-text-dark-muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .policy-content {
    max-width: 750px;
  }

  .policy-content section + section {
    margin-top: 4rem;
    padding-top: 4rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-cream-200);
  }

  .policy-content h2 {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 2.4rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .policy-content p,
  .policy-content li {
    color: var(--color-text-dark-muted);
  }

  .policy-content p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .policy-content p + p {
    margin-top: 1.2rem;
  }

  .policy-content ul {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }

  .policy-content li + li {
    margin-top: 0.45rem;
  }

  .policy-content a {
    color: var(--color-burgundy);
    text-underline-offset: 0.18em;
  }

  .contact-link {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.3rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .page-hero {
      padding: 145px 0 80px;
    }

    .policy-layout {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 4rem;
    }

    aside {
      position: static;
    }

    .policy-content section + section {
      margin-top: 3rem;
      padding-top: 3rem;
    }
  }
</style>